2. Inversion of Control (IoC)

14. What is IoC?
-->Simple definition

    IoC (Inversion of Control) means the control of creating and managing objects is transferred from the programmer 
    to the Spring container.

Normally, we create objects ourselves using new.

Engine engine = new Engine();
Car car = new Car(engine);

Here, we are controlling object creation.

With Spring:
    
    @Component
    class Engine {
    }
    @Component
    class Car {
    
        private Engine engine;
    
        @Autowired
        public Car(Engine engine) {
            this.engine = engine;
        }
    }

Now Spring creates the Engine and Car objects and injects Engine into Car.

Interview answer :
  IoC is a principle where the control of object creation, configuration, and dependency management is transferred 
    from the application code to the Spring IoC container.


Remember
Without IoC:

  Developer → creates object → manages object

With IoC:
    Developer → tells Spring what is needed
                        ↓
                  Spring Container
                        ↓
                creates & manages objects






15. Why is IoC needed?
-->IoC is mainly needed to achieve loose coupling and make applications easier to maintain and test.

Consider:

class Car {

    Engine engine = new Engine();

}

Car is directly creating Engine.

If tomorrow we want:

ElectricEngine

we have to modify Car.

With IoC:

class Car {

    private Engine engine;

    public Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
}

Now Car doesn't care which implementation of Engine it receives.

Main reasons for IoC
Loose coupling
Easier testing
Easier maintenance
Dependency management
Flexible application design
Easier replacement of implementations
Interview answer

IoC is needed to reduce tight coupling between classes, manage dependencies automatically, improve testability, and make the
application easier to maintain and extend.







16. What is IoC container?
-->The IoC Container is the component in Spring that creates, configures, stores, and manages objects.
      
      Objects managed by Spring are called beans.
      
      For example:
      
      @Component
      class Engine {
      }
      
      Spring sees @Component and creates an Engine object.
      
      The container manages its lifecycle.
      
      Two important Spring IoC containers
      BeanFactory
      ApplicationContext
      
      ApplicationContext is the commonly used one in real Spring applications.
      
      Simple flow
      @Component
          ↓
      Spring Container
          ↓
      Creates Object
          ↓
      Stores Object as Bean
          ↓
      Injects Bean wherever required
      Interview answer
      
      The Spring IoC Container is responsible for creating, configuring, managing, and injecting the dependencies of Spring beans.
      ApplicationContext is the commonly used IoC container in Spring applications.






17. How does Spring implement IoC?
-->Spring implements IoC mainly through Dependency Injection (DI).
    There are three common types of dependency injection:

1. Constructor Injection
        @Component
        class Car {
        
            private Engine engine;
        
            @Autowired
            Car(Engine engine) {
                this.engine = engine;
            }
        }    
        Spring creates Engine and passes it to Car.

2. Setter Injection
      @Component
      class Car {
      
          private Engine engine;
      
          @Autowired
          public void setEngine(Engine engine) {
              this.engine = engine;
          }
      }


3. Field Injection
      @Component
      class Car {
      
          @Autowired
          private Engine engine;
      }

Although field injection exists, constructor injection is generally preferred because dependencies are explicit and the object 
can be immutable.
      
      Important relationship
      IoC
       ↓
      Dependency Injection
       ↓
      Spring provides required objects


Strictly speaking, IoC is the broader principle; Dependency Injection is one way to implement IoC.

Interview answer :
    Spring implements IoC primarily through Dependency Injection. The container creates the required beans and injects their 
    dependencies using constructor, setter, or field injection.







18. Difference between traditional object creation and IoC?
-->This is a very common interview question.

      Traditional approach
      class Car {
      
          Engine engine = new Engine();
      
      }
      
      Car creates its own dependency.
      
      IoC approach
      class Car {
      
          private Engine engine;
      
          public Car(Engine engine) {
              this.engine = engine;
          }
      }
      
      Spring provides the Engine.


Comparison
| Traditional                            | IoC                             |
| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Developer creates objects              | Spring creates objects          |
| Uses `new` frequently                  | Container manages objects       |
| Dependencies are often tightly coupled | Dependencies are injected       |
| Harder to test                         | Easier to test                  |
| Harder to replace dependencies         | Easy to replace implementations |
| Developer manages object lifecycle     | Spring manages bean lifecycle   |

Easy way to remember
Traditional:
    "I need an Engine, so I'll create one." 
    new Engine();

IoC:
    "I need an Engine. Spring, you give me one."
    Car(Engine engine)










19. What is loose coupling?
-->Loose coupling means classes have minimum dependency on the concrete implementation of other classes.

For example, this is tightly coupled:

class Car {

    PetrolEngine engine = new PetrolEngine();

}

Car specifically depends on PetrolEngine.

If we want an electric engine, we need to change Car.

Loose coupling

Use an interface:

interface Engine {
    void start();
}

Implementations:

class PetrolEngine implements Engine {

    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Petrol engine started");
    }
}
class ElectricEngine implements Engine {

    public void start() {
        System.out.println("Electric engine started");
    }
}

Now:

class Car {

    private Engine engine;

    Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
}

Car depends on:

Engine

rather than:

PetrolEngine

That's loose coupling.

Interview answer

Loose coupling means reducing the dependency between classes so that one class depends on abstractions rather than concrete implementations.







20. How IoC supports loose coupling?
-->This is directly related to the previous question.

Without IoC:

class Car {

    Engine engine = new Engine();

}

Car is responsible for creating Engine.

With IoC:

class Car {

    private Engine engine;

    Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
}

Now Car doesn't create the dependency.

Spring provides it.

Car
 ↓
depends on
 ↓
Engine interface
 ↑
Spring injects implementation

For example:

@Component
class PetrolEngine implements Engine {
}

Spring can inject that implementation.

Tomorrow:

@Component
class ElectricEngine implements Engine {
}

The implementation can be changed/configured without making Car responsible for constructing it.

Interview answer

IoC promotes loose coupling by separating object creation from object usage. A class receives its dependencies from the
container instead of creating them itself, so it can depend on abstractions rather than concrete implementations.











21. Advantages of IoC?
-->The major advantages are:

1. Loose coupling

Classes don't directly create their dependencies.

2. Easy testing

We can provide mock objects.

Car car = new Car(mockEngine);

Instead of creating a real engine.

3. Easy maintenance

Changes to dependencies don't necessarily require changes to dependent classes.

4. Reusability

Spring-managed beans can be reused wherever needed.

5. Centralized configuration

Dependencies can be configured by the Spring container.

6. Lifecycle management

Spring manages bean creation and destruction.

7. Easy replacement of implementations

For example:

PetrolEngine
     ↓
ElectricEngine

can be switched without making Car responsible for creating either one.

Interview answer

The main advantages of IoC are loose coupling, better testability, easier maintenance, centralized dependency management, reusable
components, lifecycle management, and flexibility in 
replacing implementations.









22. Real-time example of IoC?
--->Let's take a payment application.

Suppose an application supports:

Payment
   |
   ├── UPI
   ├── Credit Card
   └── PayPal

Create an interface:

public interface Payment {

    void pay();

}

UPI implementation:  
        @Component
        public class UpiPayment implements Payment {
        
            public void pay() {
                System.out.println("Payment using UPI");
            }
        }

Payment service:
    @Component
    public class PaymentService {
    
        private Payment payment;
    
        public PaymentService(Payment payment) {
            this.payment = payment;
        }
    
        public void makePayment() {
            payment.pay();
        }
    }

Here PaymentService doesn't do:
        new UpiPayment();
        
                Spring provides the object.
                
                        Spring Container
                               ↓
                        Creates UpiPayment
                               ↓
                        Injects into PaymentService
                               ↓
                        PaymentService.makePayment()

This is a practical example of IoC.









23. Difference between IoC and Factory Pattern?  
-->Both are related to object creation, but they are not the same.

Factory Pattern

A Factory class is responsible for creating objects.

Example:

class PaymentFactory {

    public Payment getPayment(String type) {

        if (type.equals("UPI")) {
            return new UpiPayment();
        }

        return new CreditCardPayment();
    }
}

Then:

PaymentFactory factory = new PaymentFactory();

Payment payment = factory.getPayment("UPI");

We are still explicitly asking the factory for an object.

IoC

With Spring:

@Component
class PaymentService {

    private Payment payment;

    PaymentService(Payment payment) {
        this.payment = payment;
    }
}

Spring automatically creates and injects the required dependency.

| IoC                                        | Factory Pattern                      |
| ------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |
| Design principle                           | Creational design pattern            |
| Framework/container manages objects        | Factory creates objects              |
| Dependencies can be automatically injected | Usually request object from factory  |
| Manages dependency relationships           | Mainly focuses on object creation    |
| Spring Container can manage lifecycle      | Factory usually only creates objects |


Interview answer

Factory Pattern centralizes object creation in a factory class, whereas IoC transfers object creation and dependency management to a container such as the Spring Container.

⭐ Very Important: IoC vs DI vs IoC Container

Interviewers commonly ask these together, so don't confuse them.

IoC
│
│  Principle
│  "Don't create dependencies yourself"
│
↓
Dependency Injection (DI)
│
│  Technique to implement IoC
│  "Dependencies are provided from outside"
│
↓
Spring IoC Container
│
│  The actual Spring component
│  that performs the work
│
↓
Creates Beans
│
↓
Injects Dependencies
│
↓
Manages Bean Lifecycle

For example:

@Component
class Engine {
}

@Component
class Car {

    private Engine engine;

    Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;
    }
}

Think of it as:

IoC: Who controls object creation? → Spring

DI: How does Car receive Engine? → Spring injects it

IoC Container: Who actually creates and injects these objects? → Spring Container

Bean: What do we call an object managed by Spring? → Spring Bean

This concept is the foundation for understanding @Component, @Autowired, @Bean, constructor injection, and the Spring bean lifecycle.







